this question focuses to joining the opening and closing of brackets in orderly manner
suppose "()" -> i/p, then o/p is true
similarly, ([]) -> i/p, o/p is true
({)} -> i/p o/p -> false

Brute force approach: We need to assign the opening and the closing of brackets. So, the approach would be to find the pairs and remove them until nothing exists
#code:

class Solution
  def isValid(self, s: str) -> bool:
    while True:
      prev = s
      s=s.replace("()", "")
      s=s.replace("[]", "")
      s=s.replace("{}", "")

      if s == prev:
        break

  return s == ""

Space O(n) Time O(n^2)

Optimal approach: Stack

Stack follows (LIFO) - last in first out
So, the last element will be first removed. Basically like stacking plates and when the stack is empty, the brackets are valid because we are removing from the stack and matching it with the pair

#code:
class Solution:
    def isValid(self, s: str) -> bool:
        pairs = {')': '(', '}': '{', ']': '['}
        stack = []

        for ch in s:
            if ch in '([{':
                stack.append(ch)
            elif ch in pairs:
                if not stack or stack.pop() != pairs[ch]:
                    return False

        return len(stack) == 0

Space O(n) Time O(n)
